# Stage 9 — Webhooks

## Objective
Understand event-driven builds using GitHub webhooks.

A webhook lets GitHub notify a reachable endpoint when an event, such as a push, occurs. Jenkins must also be configured to respond to that event.

## Workflow
```text
Git push -> GitHub webhook -> Jenkins trigger -> Pipeline
```

## Lab outline
1. Configure a Jenkins job with the appropriate GitHub integration or webhook trigger.
2. Configure the repository webhook using the endpoint and event type required by your setup.
3. Make a test commit.
4. Inspect the webhook delivery and Jenkins build history.

A local `localhost:8080` endpoint is not reachable directly by GitHub. Use a secure, reachable endpoint or an appropriate alternative for your lab. Do not expose Jenkins without access controls.

## Assignment
Document the webhook event, endpoint configuration, and evidence that the build was triggered.
